---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout title="Linh Nguyen">
  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto pt-8 pb-16">
    <a href="/journals" class="text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400 hover:underline"
      >&larr; Back to Journals</a
    >
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold mt-8 mb-4 text-gray-900 dark:text-white">
      Whatever You Wanna Do
    </h1>
    <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 mb-8 italic">July 7, 2017</p>
    <div class="prose dark:prose-invert max-w-none space-y-4">
      <p class="text-lg">I've always loved this Norm Macdonald quote:</p>
      <blockquote>
        "It's the greatest gig in the world, being alive. You get to eat at
        Denny's, wear a hat, whatever you wanna do."
      </blockquote>
      <p class="text-lg">
        It's whimsical but it also speaks to something bigger: the simple,
        often-overlooked joys of life, and the incredible freedom we have in how
        we live it.
      </p>
      <p class="text-lg">
        Last semester, I was obsessed with the idea that time feels slower and
        richer when you break out of routine and fill your days with maximal
        variety. Rest has its place, of course, but this summer I've been
        packing days as full as I can and chasing novelty whenever possible.
        It's a sort of time travel mechanism, or life-extension mechanism. You
        can feel like you're slowing the perceived passage of time by doing more
        living.
      </p>
      <p class="text-lg">
        An extension of this idea is that there are so many ways to be an adult.
        You can get a dog, or raise a kid. You can go to college, or drop out.
        You can move across the world. You can eat exotic meats or pancakes for
        dinner. Sometimes we get so trapped in the inertia of our lives, we
        forget that there's all sorts of things you can just do if you simply
        decide to.
      </p>
      <p class="text-lg">
        In that spirit, today I woke up and randomly decided to brew a batch of
        beer. There's a big homebrewing culture in the chemical engineering
        community. It's inherently a chemical engineering process: you can model
        the fermentation in a "bioreactor" and play around with concepts like
        heat transfer and stoichiometry. Of course, you don't have to do any of
        this but I suppose these things make it more compelling to ChemEs
        specifically.
      </p>
      <p class="text-lg">
        Some full-time engineers at work mentioned that they've dabbled in
        homebrewing, but I was never particularly interested until this morning.
        Some plans fell through and suddenly I had a full empty day. On a whim,
        I texted my coworker (who probably doesn't realize I'm not 21 yet) and
        dropped by to borrow some homebrewing equipment and hops. I then went to
        a local homebrew supply shop and grabbed some yeast and liquid malt
        extract, looked up a recipe, and went for it.
      </p>

      <div class="flex justify-center gap-6 my-6">
        <img
          src="/images/brew-ingredients.png"
          alt="Brewing ingredients"
          class="max-w-xs w-full rounded"
        />
        <img
          src="/images/wort-snap.png"
          alt="Snap of wort"
          class="max-w-xs w-full rounded"
        />
      </div>

      <p class="text-lg">
        We'll see in a month (after fermenting and bottling) whether this tastes
        any good, but from what I've gathered, it's surprisingly hard to mess up
        as long as you conduct all the sanitation rigorously. Regardless of the
        outcome, it was an unexpectedly fun and rewarding way to spend an
        afternoon (and for sure beats the likely default of binging Youtube
        videos).
      </p>

      <p class="text-lg">
        Successful experiment in injecting life with a little stochasticity, and
        doing whatever you wanna do. :)
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
